<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p16_b9> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#FigureImage> <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p16_b9> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/278915007> <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p16_b10> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#caption> "The diagram illustrates the anatomy of the pelvic region, showing the organs and structures within the lower abdomen and pelvis." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p16_b9> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The diagram features a yellow oval shape centrally placed in the lower abdomen, representing the bladder. Extending from the bladder towards the right side is a gray, wavy line, symbolizing the bowel. Below the bladder sits a small, oval-shaped structure, the prostate. At the front of the pelvis, a curved, light blue structure indicates the pubic bone. A red, straight line, the urethra, runs from the bladder to the external opening. Beneath the bladder and urethra lies a red, horizontal band, depicting the pelvic floor muscles. At the bottom right, a small, circular opening marks the anus. On the right side, a series of stacked, white, rectangular shapes form the spine. Each part is connected by a black line or arrow pointing to the corresponding anatomical feature, set against a light background with different colors for various organs and tissues." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p16_b9> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/53505006> <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p16_b10> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#figureRef> "c022" <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p16_b10> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/818983003> <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p15_b2> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/12921003> <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p16_b10> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/12921003> <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p15_b2> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The illustration features a large, curved bone at the top, likely part of the pelvic girdle, with a smooth, yellowish surface and shading for depth. Below this bone, two blue, rounded structures with a glossy appearance are positioned, possibly representing organs or glands. These blue structures are connected by a small green structure that resembles a duct or tube. Beneath the blue structures, a yellowish curved line is present, potentially indicating a ligament or another anatomical feature. On the right side of the image, vertical text reading \"Illustration by Smart-Servier Medical Art\" is aligned along the edge." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p15_b2> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#figureRef> "c022" <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p29_b10> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/7295002> <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p16_b9> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/818983003> <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p24_b10> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/12921003> <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p16_b9> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/12921003> <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p24_b10> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The illustration is divided into two sections: the left side displays the male pelvic region, while the right side shows the female pelvic region." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p29_b10> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The image is divided into two main sections. On the left, the male pelvic area is shown with the pelvic bones in light beige, and the perineal muscles are colored in green for the Transverse perineal muscles, purple for the Ischiocavernosus muscle, pink for the Bulbospongiosus muscle, yellow for the Anus, blue for the External anal sphincter, and a small portion of the Puborectalis muscle in green. On the right, the female pelvic area is similarly depicted with the same color scheme for the muscles, but it also includes pink for the Vagina and red for the Penis. At the bottom, a legend explains the colors: green for Transverse perineal muscles, purple for Ischiocavernosus, pink for Bulbospongiosus, yellow for Anus, blue for External anal sphincter, green for Puborectalis, pink for Iliococcygeus part of levator ani muscle, orange for Penis, and light orange for Vagina. The title \"PELVIC FLOOR MUSCLE\" is centered at the top." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p24_b10> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#figureRef> "c022" <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p16_b9> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#figureRef> "c022" <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p29_b10> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/102290008> <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p29_b10> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#caption> "The figure illustrates the pelvic floor muscles in males and females, highlighting the differences in perineal muscles from a posterior view." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p16_b9> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/113276009> <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s5> .
