<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p19_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#caption> "The figure displays two anatomical diagrams of the pelvic region, each offering a distinct perspective." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p16_b9> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#caption> "A diagram illustrating the anatomy of the lower abdomen and pelvic area, including the bladder, bowel, prostate, pubic bone, urethra, pelvic floor muscles, anus, coccyx, and spine." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p16_b10> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The diagram features a yellow, oval-shaped bladder positioned in the lower abdomen, near the pubic bone. A red, curved urethra extends from the bladder towards the front. The pubic bone is depicted as a light blue, curved structure at the front of the pelvis. Above the bladder sits a pink, pear-shaped uterus. To the right and upwards from the bladder, a gray, coiled bowel is shown. At the bottom of the diagram, a red, horizontal band represents the pelvic floor muscles, spanning the pelvis. The anus is a small, dark opening at the bottom right. The coccyx is a small, triangular structure at the base of the spine, which is represented by a series of white, stacked rectangular structures on the right side of the diagram. Each part is labeled with a leader line or arrow pointing to the corresponding anatomical feature." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s5> .
