<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p16_b10> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The anterior superior iliac spine is positioned at the top left corner of the image, pointing to a bony prominence on the pelvis. The external oblique muscle is depicted in a reddish-pink color, covering the upper portion of the image, with its fibers running diagonally across the abdominal wall. The aponeurosis of the external oblique appears as a white, fibrous layer extending downward from the muscle. The inguinal ligament is a band of tissue running horizontally across the lower abdomen, connecting the anterior superior iliac spine to the pubic tubercle. The lacunar ligament is a small, triangular structure located within the inguinal canal, just below the inguinal ligament. The femoral artery and vein are shown in red and blue, respectively, within the inguinal canal, positioned centrally in the lower part of the image. The pubic tubercle is a bony prominence on the pubic bone, located at the bottom right of the image." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s11> .
