<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p34_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The image presents a detailed anatomical illustration of the male abdominal wall and inguinal region. At the top, the peritoneum is depicted as a thin layer. Beneath it, the transversalis fascia is shown, followed by the transversus abdominis muscle. The internal oblique muscle is portrayed in a darker red color below the transversus abdominis, while the external oblique muscle is shown in a lighter red as the outermost layer. Blood vessels like the testicular artery and veins are illustrated entering the spermatic cord, alongside the ductus deferens and the ilio-inguinal nerve. The inferior epigastric vessels are depicted supplying the lower abdominal wall. The deep inguinal ring is shown as an opening through which the spermatic cord passes, with extraperitoneal fat located behind it. Intercrural fibers are illustrated connecting the inner thigh muscles. The internal spermatic fascia is shown surrounding the spermatic cord, while the inguinal ligament runs horizontally across the inguinal region. The origin of the cremasteric muscle is marked, and the inguinal falx (conjoint tendon) is depicted as a triangular fold. Femoral vessels are shown passing through the femoral canal, and the superficial inguinal ring is illustrated as an opening in the superficial fascia. The external spermatic fascia is also depicted surrounding the spermatic cord." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s11> .
