<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p36_b9> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The external oblique muscle is positioned at the top left of the image, with its fibers running diagonally across the abdominal wall in a reddish-pink color. Near the top left corner, slightly below the external oblique muscle, the anterior superior iliac spine is marked with a label pointing to the bony prominence of the pelvis. The aponeurosis of the external oblique is located at the top right, shown as a blue, fibrous layer extending from the external oblique muscle. The inguinal ligament is positioned horizontally below the anterior superior iliac spine, depicted as a thin, blue line connecting the pubic tubercle to the spine. In the middle of the image, slightly below the inguinal ligament, the superficial inguinal ring is shown as an opening in the aponeurosis of the external oblique muscle. Within this ring, the spermatic cord is positioned, depicted as a bundle of structures extending downward into the scrotum. At the bottom of the image, the femoral artery and vein are shown as two parallel structures, with the vein on the left and the artery on the right. The femur is visible at the bottom, extending from the hip joint down to the knee in a light brown color. The image uses a color scheme—red for muscles, blue for ligaments and connective tissues, and beige for bones—to differentiate between various anatomical structures, with leader lines connecting labels to specific parts." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s11> .
