<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p33_b8> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The linea alba is positioned at the top-center of the image as a thin white line running vertically down the center of the abdominal wall. The anterior superior iliac spine is marked on the left side, indicating a bony prominence on the pelvis. Near the bottom-center, the deep inguinal ring is shown as a small opening in the abdominal wall muscle, situated just above the pubic bone. Below the deep inguinal ring, the superficial inguinal ring is depicted as another opening in the abdominal wall muscle, located slightly higher. The external oblique muscle is displayed on the right side in a reddish-pink color, appearing as a broad, flat muscle extending from the lower ribs to the pubic bone. The aponeurosis of the external oblique is also on the right, shown as a blue, web-like structure extending from the lower ribs to the pubic bone. The inguinal ligament is positioned on the right, running horizontally across the lower abdomen, connecting the anterior superior iliac spine to the pubic tubercle. The spermatic cord is illustrated on the right as a red and blue tube-like structure, indicating a bundle of nerves, blood vessels, and the vas deferens that pass through the inguinal canal. The bones of the pelvis are visible, providing context for the location of the inguinal area." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s11> .
