<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p34_b2> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The illustration presents an anterior-inferior view of the inguinal region, with the anterior superior iliac spine positioned at the top-left corner. The aponeurosis of the external oblique spans across the top of the image, extending from the anterior superior iliac spine. Near this aponeurosis, the lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh is visible. The inguinal ligament runs horizontally across the lower abdomen, connecting the anterior inferior iliac spine to the pubic tubercle. The femoral nerve, femoral artery, and femoral vein are depicted running parallel to each other. The anterior inferior iliac spine is situated below the anterior superior iliac spine. The psoas major muscle is partially visible deep within the pelvis. At the bottom of the image, the acetabulum is shown. The femoral canal is illustrated as a passage for the femoral vessels, with the pectineus muscle situated within it. The pubic tubercle is positioned at the bottom-right of the image, near the superficial inguinal ring, which is a circular opening in the abdominal wall." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s11> .
