<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/75a8c84d58f0> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#subject> <http://snomed.info/id/414403008> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s13> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p60_b12> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#caption> "The figure illustrates the detailed structure of the human large intestine, including its various sections and bends." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s13> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p60_b12> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The illustration presents the large intestine as a continuous loop, with the right colic flexure positioned at the top left, pointing to the upper right side. The transverse colon stretches horizontally across the middle, while the left colic flexure is at the top right, pointing to the lower left. The ascending colon curves upwards on the left side, and the descending colon curves downwards on the right. The sigmoid colon connects the descending colon to the rectum, which is depicted as a straight, narrow tube at the bottom. The anal canal is shown as the final segment leading to the anus. Additional features include the omental appendage extending vertically from the ascending colon, the haustra of the colon appearing as pouch-like structures along the ascending and descending sections, and the taeniae coli, which are three longitudinal muscle bands running along the colon. The cecum is portrayed as a pouch-like structure connected to the ascending colon, with the appendix, a small, finger-like projection, attached to it." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s13> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/assert/7a6d4014d851> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#target> <http://snomed.info/id/280591001> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s13> .
