<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p7_b11> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The illustration provides a side view of the head and neck, with the skull and mandible prominently displayed. The trachea, clavicle, and manubrium of the sternum are clearly visible, along with the hyoid bone and thyroid cartilage. Leader lines connect the labels to the corresponding structures in the image." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_neck/s6> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p58_b17> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#caption> "The figure illustrates the muscles and anatomical structures of the neck and upper back, including the m. sternocleidomastoideus, processus mastoideus, m. scalenus medius, m. scalenus anterior, m. omohyoideus (venter inferior), Occipital triangle, m. trapezius, and clavícula." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_neck/s6> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p49_b2> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The image presents a cross-sectional view of the neck and shoulder, with labels positioned near the corresponding anatomical structures. The anterior midline of the neck is shown as a central vertical line, while the sternocleidomastoid muscles are depicted on either side. The trapezius muscle is illustrated broadly on the back of the neck and shoulders. The anterior triangle is marked as a triangular area on the front side of the neck, bordered by the anterior midline, the sternocleidomastoid, and the trapezius. The posterior triangle is similarly shown as a triangular area on the back side, formed by the posterior midline, the sternocleidomastoid, and the trapezius. The axillary inlet is indicated at the base of the neck, where the axilla begins, and the clavicle is shown as a horizontal bone across the chest. The superior thoracic aperture is labeled at the top of the ribcage." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_neck/s6> .
