<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p39_b9> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "At the top left, a lighter, fibrous region represents the interlobular connective tissue. Scattered throughout the image are clusters of cells with larger, centrally located nuclei, which are the cells of pancreatic islets. Near the bottom left, a small, circular structure is labeled as an intercalated duct. The main acinar cells, characterized by their polygonal shape and smaller, more peripheral nuclei, dominate the tissue and are distributed across most of the image. Towards the top right, a larger duct structure labeled as the interlobular duct is visible. A specialized sensory structure, the Pacinian corpuscle, is located near the top right. Another centroacinar cell is labeled near the middle right, and another blood vessel, similar in appearance to the first, is situated near the middle right. Within the pancreatic islets, small, thin-walled vessels labeled as capillaries are present." <urn:graph:histology_endocrine/s8> .
