<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p69_b3> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The aorta is positioned at the top left, extending vertically downward. The celiac artery branches off from the aorta, pointing toward the center-left. The common hepatic artery branches off from the celiac artery, directing slightly downward and to the right. The gastrointestinal artery branches off from the common hepatic artery, pointing downward and to the right. The right gastro-omental artery branches off from the gastrointestinal artery, also pointing downward and to the right. The dorsal pancreatic artery branches off from the celiac artery, pointing slightly downward and to the right. The splenic artery branches off from the celiac artery, pointing slightly downward and to the right. The spleen is shown on the right side of the image, with its surface texture and internal structure visible in light purple. The greater pancreatic artery branches off from the splenic artery, pointing downward and to the right. The inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery branches off from the greater pancreatic artery, pointing downward and to the right, then dividing into anterior and posterior branches. The anterior and posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries branch off from the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery, pointing downward and to the right. The uncinate process of the pancreas is located below the pancreas, pointing toward the lower center. The superior mesenteric artery is located at the bottom center, extending upward. Each artery is labeled with a black arrow pointing to the specific part of the anatomy it supplies. The other structures are depicted in shades of yellow and red, representing different tissues and blood vessels." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s10> .
