<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p81_b7> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The gallbladder is positioned at the top left of the image, with its three parts labeled: Fundus, Body, and Neck. The cystic duct is a small tube extending downward and slightly to the right from the gallbladder, connecting it to the common bile duct. The common bile duct, a larger tube, is also labeled near the bottom of the gallbladder, extending downward and slightly to the right. Near the top right, the right and left hepatic ducts are shown branching off from the common hepatic duct, which is formed by their confluence. On the right side of the image, the pancreatic ducts are labeled, with the accessory duct branching off the main pancreatic duct. At the bottom, the duodenum, a large curved section of the small intestine, is depicted surrounding the area where the bile duct and pancreatic ducts enter. Numbers 1, 2, and 3 are present, likely indicating vascularization, with the cystic artery originating from the right hepatic artery." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s10> .
