<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p11_b5> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The diagram is divided into a left and right side, with labels indicating the direction from superficial to deep. On the left side, the outermost layer is labeled as 'Skin (cut edge)', followed by the 'Superficial fatty layer of subcutaneous tissue (Camper fascia)', then the 'Deep membranous layer of subcutaneous tissue (Scarpa fascia)', and finally the 'Investing (deep) fascia—superficial, intermediate, and deep' at the bottom. On the right side, the layers are labeled from top to bottom as 'External oblique', 'Internal oblique', 'Transversus abdominis', 'Extraperitoneal fat', 'Endoabdominal (transversalis) fascia', and 'Parietal peritoneum'. The terms 'Superficial' and 'Deep' are positioned at the top left and right corners respectively, with arrows pointing towards the corresponding sides of the diagram. The layers are color-coded for clarity, though specific colors are not described." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/642250e5edbd> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#subject> <http://snomed.info/id/39937001> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p12_b5> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#caption> "A labeled diagram of a longitudinal section of the abdominal wall, showing layers from superficial to deep." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p13_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The image presents a detailed anatomical illustration of the abdominal wall, with the skin depicted as the outermost layer in light brown. Beneath the skin, the superficial fascia is divided into a fatty layer (Camper's fascia) shown in yellow with a wavy texture, and a membranous layer (Scarpa's fascia) in a lighter yellow. The external oblique muscle appears as a red layer on the right side, while the internal oblique muscle is positioned below it in green. The transversus abdominis muscle, also in red, is located between the internal oblique and the transversalis fascia, which is a thin white layer. The parietal peritoneum is shown as a translucent pinkish layer, and the extraperitoneal fascia is white beneath it. Each structure is connected by a black line to its corresponding label, with labels placed to the left and right of the central section. The diagram is symmetrical, displaying superficial layers on the left and deeper structures on the right." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s2> .
