<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p23_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The figure is divided into three sections labeled A, B, and C. Part A provides a superior view of a transverse section through the abdominal wall, with layers color-coded: green for the external oblique muscle, orange for the internal oblique muscle, blue for the transversus abdominis muscle, black for the transversalis fascia, gray for the parietal peritoneum, and purple for the membranous layer of subcutaneous tissue. Leader lines in this section point to the rectus abdominis, linea alba, and the aponeuroses of the external oblique, internal oblique, and transversus abdominis muscles. The investing fascia is shown divided into deep, intermediate, and superficial layers, with the skin and superficial fatty layer of subcutaneous tissue also labeled. Part B presents another transverse section similar to Part A, with leader lines pointing to the rectus sheath, linea alba, and the aponeuroses of the external and internal oblique muscles. The deep membranous layer of subcutaneous tissue is also labeled here. Part C is a schematic sagittal section of the abdominal wall, with leader lines indicating the diaphragm, termination of the internal thoracic artery, peritoneum, transversalis fascia, deep membranous layer of subcutaneous tissue (Scarpa fascia), external iliac artery, body of pubis, membranous layer of subcutaneous tissue (Colles fascia), dartos fascia, and scrotum. A legend at the bottom left explains the color-coding used in all three parts." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/assert/776498355c54> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#target> <http://snomed.info/id/59380008> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/assert/bc3fa2d6eef8> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#polarity> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#Uncertain> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p8_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/85756007> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/assert/75dd712268e7> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#polarity> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#Uncertain> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p7_b9> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The illustration presents a detailed cross-section of the human abdomen, with the abdominal area cut open to reveal the internal structures. The rectus abdominis muscle is centrally positioned, extending vertically from the lower chest to the pubic area, and is colored orange. Surrounding it is the posterior wall of the rectus sheath, depicted in blue and gray. On the outer side of the abdominal wall, the external oblique muscle extends diagonally across the abdomen, also in orange. Beneath the external oblique lies the internal oblique, similarly extending diagonally and colored orange. The transversus abdominis muscle is positioned beneath the internal oblique, extending horizontally across the abdomen and colored orange as well. The transversalis fascia is shown surrounding the transversus abdominis, depicted in blue and gray. Beneath the skin, the superficial fascia is illustrated with a fatty layer (Camper's) in yellow and a membranous layer (Scarpa's) in light blue. The arcuate line is marked as a curved black line at the level of the umbilicus. The extraperitoneal fascia is shown surrounding the abdominal organs, in blue and gray, while the parietal peritoneum is depicted covering the abdominal organs in light blue. The labels are positioned around the image, pointing to their corresponding structures." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/assert/96be3e54e04e> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#ClinicalAssertion> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p11_b5> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#caption> "A labeled diagram of a longitudinal section through the abdominal wall, illustrating the layers from superficial to deep." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/e9aefd579f4e> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#extractedBy> "llm-re" <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p8_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The diagram is divided into two main sections: \"Superficial\" on the left and \"Deep\" on the right. The superficial section starts with the skin at the top, shown as a thin pink layer, followed by the superficial fatty layer of subcutaneous tissue (Camper fascia) depicted in yellow, then the deep membranous layer of subcutaneous tissue (Scarpa fascia) in darker yellow, and finally the investing fascia, which includes three parts shown as thin, layered structures. The deep section begins with the external oblique muscle on the far left, followed by the internal oblique, then the transversus abdominis, all shown in red. Between these muscle layers is the extraperitoneal fat, shown in yellow, above which is the endoabdominal (transversalis) fascia in light blue, and at the bottom, the parietal peritoneum, also in light blue. Each label is connected by a leader line or arrow pointing to the corresponding part of the diagram, with labels placed to the left of the \"Superficial\" section and to the right of the \"Deep\" section, aligned vertically." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/assert/0969bd1885f4> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#about> <http://snomed.info/id/255292000> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/assert/203aad44b051> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#polarity> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#Uncertain> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/assert/6ff7a37397fe> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#ClinicalAssertion> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/3c7c35a026c4> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#predicate> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#composed_of> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s2> .
