<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p13_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#caption> "The figure illustrates the layers and structures of the abdominal wall, including the skin, superficial fascia, external and internal oblique muscles, transversus abdominis muscle, and deeper fascial layers." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/assert/bc3fa2d6eef8> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#ClinicalAssertion> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p12_b5> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The diagram is divided into two main sections: the left side for superficial layers and the right side for deep layers. At the top-left corner, the word \"Superficial\" is written with an arrow pointing left. The left side of the diagram includes labels for \"Skin (cut edge),\" \"Superficial fatty layer of subcutaneous tissue (Camper fascia),\" and \"Deep membranous layer of subcutaneous tissue (Scarpa fascia),\" each pointing to its respective layer. At the top-right corner, the word \"Deep\" is written with an arrow pointing right. The right side of the diagram includes labels for \"External oblique,\" \"Internal oblique,\" \"Transversus abdominis,\" \"Extraperitoneal fat,\" \"Endoabdominal (transversalis) fascia,\" and \"Parietal peritoneum,\" each pointing to its respective layer. The bottom of the image reads \"Longitudinal section,\" indicating the orientation. Each layer is color-coded and connected with leader lines to corresponding text, transitioning smoothly from the skin and fatty tissue on the left to the muscle and fascial layers on the right." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/assert/75dd712268e7> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#ClinicalAssertion> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s2> .
