<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p62_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The diagram is organized into sections. At the top, the hypothalamus is shown, with blue nerve impulses traveling downward to the posterior pituitary gland. In the middle section, the posterior pituitary gland is depicted receiving these blue nerve impulses. A green arrow points from the posterior pituitary gland to the bloodstream, indicating the release of ADH. In the lower section, three target tissues are shown: a kidney on the left, a sudoriferous gland in the middle, and an arteriole on the right. Green arrows connect the bloodstream to each of these target tissues. Below the diagram, numbered steps explain the effects of ADH, with step 3 describing the liberation of ADH into the bloodstream and step 4 detailing its effects on the kidneys, sweat glands, and arterioles. At the bottom, there is a reference to the source of the image." <urn:graph:endocrine/s3> .
