<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p18_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The image is divided into three main sections, each labeled at the top. The first section on the left is labeled \"Protein and Peptide Hormones,\" featuring an illustration of a prohormone with a long chain of blue circles connected by lines, representing amino acids. Below the prohormone, two specific hormones are shown: Endothelin, a smaller chain of blue circles ending in an \"NH2\" group, and Insulin, a more complex structure with two chains labeled \"A Chain\" and \"B Chain,\" connected by a loop. The second section in the center is labeled \"Steroid Hormones,\" showing a detailed chemical structure of cholesterol, depicted as a steroid nucleus with a hydroxyl group (-OH) on one side and a branched alkane group on the other. Below the cholesterol structure, there is another chemical structure labeled \"Steroid Nucleus.\" The third section on the right is labeled \"Tyrosine Derivatives,\" displaying two chemical structures: Thyroxine, a compound with iodine atoms (-I) attached to a benzene ring linked to a tyrosine derivative, and Epinephrine, a compound with hydroxyl groups (-OH), a methoxy group (-OCH3), and a methylamine group (-NHCH3). Arrows point from the prohormone to the specific hormones and from cholesterol to the steroid nucleus, indicating the transformation process." <urn:graph:endocrine/s3> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/assert/67f26888112d> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#target> <http://snomed.info/id/116566001> <urn:graph:endocrine/s3> .
