<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p19_b2> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#caption> "A diagram illustrating a signaling pathway where a nonsteroid hormone interacts with a target cell, activating an enzyme to generate cAMP and affect cellular functions." <urn:graph:endocrine/s3> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/assert/e4176d5c8873> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#about> <http://snomed.info/id/4421005> <urn:graph:endocrine/s3> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p20_b2> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "A diagram depicts the process of steroid hormone action within a cell. A yellow-orange oval labeled \"Steroid hormone\" is positioned on the left side of the image. Near it, a pink oval labeled \"Receptor protein\" is shown. Inside the nucleus, a pink oval labeled \"Hormone-receptor complex\" is present. The nucleus also contains a double helix structure labeled \"DNA.\" Extending from the DNA is a pink wavy line labeled \"mRNA.\" Outside the nucleus, near the plasma membrane, a chain of purple dots labeled \"New protein\" is displayed. The plasma membrane is represented by a light blue area at the bottom left, while the cytoplasm is a light yellow area at the bottom right. Arrows guide the flow from the steroid hormone to the receptor protein, then to the hormone-receptor complex, which interacts with the DNA to produce mRNA, leading to the creation of new proteins in the cytoplasm." <urn:graph:endocrine/s3> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p6_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The image is divided into three sections by vertical lines, each depicting a different type of secretion. On the left, autocrine secretion is shown with a single cell and green circles representing chemical messengers, with arrows pointing from the cell's surface to its own receptors, which are also green circles. The word \"receptors\" is written at the bottom-left corner, pointing to these circles. In the middle section, paracrine secretion is illustrated with multiple cells, green circles for chemical messengers, and arrows pointing from one cell to the receptors of adjacent cells. The term \"chemical messenger\" is written above the green circles, and \"receptors\" is written below the circles on the adjacent cells. On the right, endocrine secretion is depicted with a cell releasing chemical messengers into a capillary. Arrows show the messengers moving from the cell through the basement membrane into the capillary. The terms \"endothelial cells,\" \"basement membrane,\" \"target cells in remote tissue,\" and \"capillary\" are labeled near their respective components. The diagrams consistently use green for chemical messengers, red for receptors, and blue for target cells." <urn:graph:endocrine/s3> .
