<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p5_b2> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "At the top-left, there is a gray oval shape labeled \"endocrine cell,\" featuring a darker gray nucleus-like area inside. Center-top, the word \"hormone\" appears in pink text, pointing to three red dots within the endocrine cell. A wavy pink line runs horizontally across the image, representing the bloodstream, with the label \"bloodstream\" positioned below it. To the right-center, a gray oval labeled \"receptor\" contains a blue region, indicating a receptor site. At the right-bottom, another gray oval labeled \"target cell\" also contains a blue region, similar to the receptor. Arrows extend from the red dots in the endocrine cell to the bloodstream, indicating the release of hormones. Dashed arrows connect the bloodstream to the receptor on the target cell, suggesting the movement of hormones through the bloodstream. Solid arrows point from the receptor to the target cell, indicating the interaction between the hormone and the receptor leading to a response in the target cell." <urn:graph:endocrine/s3> .
