<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p34_b2> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#caption> "The figure illustrates the anatomical structures and functional pathways of the hypothalamus and pituitary gland, focusing on the hypothalamic-pituitary axis and the hypophyseal portal system." <urn:graph:endocrine/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/9af1fde56efe> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#subject> <http://snomed.info/id/38254008> <urn:graph:endocrine/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/9af1fde56efe> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#Statement> <urn:graph:endocrine/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p3_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "A side view of a human body is presented, with different glands labeled and highlighted in close-up views. The pituitary gland is positioned at the top of the brain, near the base of the skull, while the pineal gland is situated above it, also near the base of the brain. The thyroid gland appears as a butterfly-shaped organ in the neck, below the larynx, with the parathyroid glands located on its back. The adrenal glands are depicted as two triangular glands, each situated on a kidney. The thymus is shown as a small organ behind the breastbone in the upper chest area. The pancreas is portrayed as a long, flat organ behind the stomach, extending from the upper to the lower abdomen. In females, the ovaries are illustrated as two structures in the lower abdomen, on either side of the uterus, while in males, the testes are shown as two structures in the scrotum, hanging below the penis. Each gland is connected by a leader line or arrow pointing to the corresponding part of the body. The diagram is titled \"ENDOCRINE SYSTEM\" at the bottom left corner, and the artist's signature and the year 2006 are located at the bottom right corner." <urn:graph:endocrine/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/88af477f7719> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#confidence> "0.9"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#double> <urn:graph:endocrine/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/c4813bbb7943> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#extractedBy> "llm-re" <urn:graph:endocrine/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/9af1fde56efe> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#extractedBy> "llm-re" <urn:graph:endocrine/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/f8d1cdd7eeff> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#Statement> <urn:graph:endocrine/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p71_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The illustration presents an anatomical view of the human endocrine system, with different glands and organs labeled and connected by lines to their respective body parts. The hypothalamus is shown at the base of the brain, while the pineal gland is positioned near it. The parathyroid glands are depicted on the posterior surface of the thyroid gland, which itself is located in the neck below the larynx. The heart is placed in the chest cavity, and the kidneys are shown on either side of the spine in the lower back. The pituitary gland is positioned at the base of the brain, with its anterior and posterior lobes distinctly labeled. The thymus is located in the upper chest behind the sternum, and the adrenal glands are shown above the kidneys, each subdivided into the adrenal medulla and adrenal cortex." <urn:graph:endocrine/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/a73af8261e58> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#confidence> "0.9"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#double> <urn:graph:endocrine/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/da0912e2fc4c> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#confidence> "0.9"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#double> <urn:graph:endocrine/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p70_b2> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "At the top of the diagram, neurosecretory cells are depicted with pink dots, indicating the presence of hormones. A green blood vessel is shown connected to these neurosecretory cells. Below the blood vessel, there is a label pointing to the neurosecretory cells, indicating that these cells release hormones into the blood vessel. The pituitary gland is positioned below the hypothalamus, containing yellowish structures representing the endocrine cells of the anterior pituitary. On the right side of the diagram, a list of pituitary hormones is provided: TSH, ACTH, FSH, LH, Growth hormone (GH), Prolactin (PRL), and Endorphins. Arrows connect the hypothalamus to the anterior pituitary and further arrows lead to the target organs for each hormone, which are listed on the right side. The layout clearly shows the flow of hormones from the hypothalamus to the pituitary and then to the target organs." <urn:graph:endocrine/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p22_b2> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/113343008> <urn:graph:endocrine/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p3_b1> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#FigureImage> <urn:graph:endocrine/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/4991d1ecc83b> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#Statement> <urn:graph:endocrine/s2> .
