<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/e66e3afe4a4f> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#subject> <http://snomed.info/id/23451007> <urn:graph:endocrine/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p70_b2> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#caption> "The diagram illustrates the hypothalamic-pituitary axis, showing the flow of hormones from the hypothalamus to the pituitary gland and their respective target organs." <urn:graph:endocrine/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/0c057c2f335a> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#subject> <http://snomed.info/id/264483005> <urn:graph:endocrine/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/9e05f1e66734> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#predicate> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#secretes> <urn:graph:endocrine/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p57_b2> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The diagram features a green neuron-like structure at the top, representing the hypothalamus, with a cell body containing small red dots labeled \"Hormone is made in cell body of neuron.\" An elongated projection extends downward from the hypothalamus, containing vesicles with red dots, labeled \"Vesicles containing hormone are stored in posterior pituitary.\" Below this, a blue structure represents the posterior pituitary, where the vesicles enter. At the bottom of the diagram, a blue vein is shown with arrows indicating the release of hormones into the blood, labeled \"Hormones are released into blood.\" Arrows connect these labeled parts, showing the flow from the hypothalamus to the posterior pituitary and into the bloodstream." <urn:graph:endocrine/s2> .
