<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/7a0e86243d6d> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#Statement> <urn:graph:digestive_system/s6> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p13_b6> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The image presents a table with a teal-colored header labeled \"Stomach.\" The table is structured into four rows and two columns. The table is organized without leader lines or arrows, ensuring clarity in the information presented." <urn:graph:digestive_system/s6> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p32_b3> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The diagram is organized into four main sections, each connected by arrows indicating flow. At the top left, there's a text box that provides an overview of the liver's role in processing blood components. On the left side, a section labeled \"Absorbed from gastrointestinal tract\" lists items like bilirubin, nutrients, drugs, and foreign substances, which are connected to the liver via the hepatic portal vein. Below this, the \"Secreted into duodenum\" section lists bile salts, bilirubin, water, ions, and phospholipids, connected to the liver by the bile duct. The central section, labeled \"Liver,\" outlines its functions, including glucose and fat metabolism, protein synthesis, hormone synthesis, urea production, detoxification, and storage. To the right, the \"Metabolites and drugs from peripheral tissues\" section lists bilirubin, metabolites of hormones and drugs, and nutrients, connected to the liver by the hepatic artery." <urn:graph:digestive_system/s6> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/7a0e86243d6d> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#subject> <http://snomed.info/id/70150004> <urn:graph:digestive_system/s6> .
