<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p54_b6> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/26671000> <urn:graph:histology_digestive_1/s2> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p48_b6> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The diagram is divided into two sections: a larger depiction showing a side view of the tooth and gum with labels pointing to different anatomical features, and a smaller inset providing a close-up of the junctional epithelium and the free gingival groove. The gingival sulcus is shown as a small groove between the tooth and the gum, while the margin of the gingiva is the edge of the gum tissue. The free gingiva is depicted as the portion not attached to the tooth, and the free gingival groove is a small groove on this free gingiva. The attached gingiva is shown as the part firmly attached to the tooth, with the junctional epithelium at the junction of the gum and tooth. Circular fibers are illustrated surrounding the tooth, and dento-gingival fibers are shown connecting the tooth to the gum. Dentin is portrayed as the hard tissue beneath the enamel. Periodontal ligaments are depicted holding the tooth in place. The diagram also includes labels for acellular and cellular cementum, periodontal membrane with Sharpey's fibers, epithelial rest of Mallasez, and alveolar bone." <urn:graph:histology_digestive_1/s2> .
