<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p52_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "A simplified human silhouette is positioned on the left side of the image, with the reproductive organs, the uterus and ovaries, highlighted within it. At the top of the image, the section labeled \"Hypothalamic neurosecretory cells\" is connected by lines to the \"Pituitary portal system\" below, with the \"Amygdala\" also labeled and connected to the hypothalamus. Hormones are represented by arrows moving from the hypothalamus to the anterior pituitary and then to the ovary. Specific hormones are labeled: GnRH (Gonadotropin-releasing hormone) with arrows pointing from the hypothalamus to the anterior pituitary, LH (Luteinizing hormone) and FSH (Follicle-stimulating hormone) with arrows pointing from the anterior pituitary to the ovary. Estradiol and Progesterone are shown moving from the ovary to the hypothalamus, with arrows pointing back. The ovary is depicted at the bottom right, connected to the anterior pituitary by arrows labeled with FSH and LH. Different colors are used to represent hormones: red dots for Estradiol, blue squares for FSH, and orange triangles for LH." <urn:graph:endocrine/s8> .
