<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p41_b10> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The illustration focuses on the neck region, where the external carotid artery is positioned on the left side, branching from the common carotid artery. The superior thyroid artery and vein are depicted alongside the internal carotid artery, with the vein running alongside. The thyrohyoid membrane is shown as a connective tissue between the thyroid gland and the hyoid bone. The internal jugular vein is a large vein on the right side, draining blood from the head and neck. The middle thyroid vein is a smaller vein connecting to the thyroid gland, while the inferior thyroid veins are shown draining blood from the lower part of the thyroid gland. The transverse cervical artery and suprascapular artery are depicted as small arteries crossing the neck and near the shoulder blade, respectively. The subclavian artery and vein are shown on the right side. The brachiocephalic trunk and brachiocephalic veins are also depicted, with the trunk being the main artery supplying blood to the upper body and the veins draining blood from the upper body." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_endocrine_glands/s8> .
