<http://example.org/medkg/inst/assert/d13721c7b3c3> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#target> <http://snomed.info/id/3057000> <urn:graph:histology_digestive_1/s4> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p43_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The diagram presents a detailed cross-section of a tooth, with labels positioned around the periphery. The outermost layer, enamel, is situated at the top left, transitioning into the dentinoenamel junction just below it. Dentin, the main body of the tooth, occupies most of the cross-section, with interglobular spaces visible as gaps within it. At the bottom left, the cementum surrounds the root of the tooth. Curved lines in the dentin, the lines of Schreger, run parallel to the dentinal tubules. Each label is connected by a leader line or arrow to its corresponding part in the diagram." <urn:graph:histology_digestive_1/s4> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p49_b7> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/38199008> <urn:graph:histology_digestive_1/s4> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/assert/fa84ae7bf12e> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#polarity> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#Uncertain> <urn:graph:histology_digestive_1/s4> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p53_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#caption> "The figure illustrates the progressive stages of tooth development from an initial primordium to a fully formed tooth with its various layers and structures." <urn:graph:histology_digestive_1/s4> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p29_b6> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The diagram presents a cross-sectional view of the human head and neck, with the nasal cavity positioned at the top left. An arrow points downward from the nasal cavity to the hard palate, which is situated above the oral cavity. Another arrow connects the hard palate to the oral cavity, and from there, an arrow leads downward to the soft palate. The lips are shown on the left side, while the tongue is centrally positioned within the oral cavity. The jaw is depicted on the lower left side, and the pharynx is to the right of the soft palate. Below the pharynx, an arrow points to the epiglottis, and at the bottom left, the mylohyoid muscle is indicated. The diagram uses arrows to connect labels to specific anatomical parts, clearly defining the relationships between these structures." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_face_and_oral_cavity/s7> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/bece64cc0ab6> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#object> <http://snomed.info/id/48477009> <urn:graph:anatomy_of_face_and_oral_cavity/s7> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p27_b9> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/32849002> <urn:graph:anatomy_of_face_and_oral_cavity/s7> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p27_b9> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/263352000> <urn:graph:anatomy_of_face_and_oral_cavity/s7> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p51_b12> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The diagram presents a cross-section of the mouth, with the teeth and gums clearly visible. The lips, palate, and pharynx are also depicted. Labial glands are positioned at the top of the image, near the upper lip, while palatine glands are in the middle, pointing towards the soft palate. Pharyngeal glands are at the bottom, directed towards the pharynx. All glands are highlighted in yellow and labeled accordingly." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_face_and_oral_cavity/s7> .
