<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p48_b14> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The illustration presents a detailed view of the neck and upper chest, with various anatomical structures labeled and connected by leader lines. The masseter muscle is positioned at the top left, near the cheek area, while the parotid gland is situated beside it, closer to the side of the face. The platysma muscle is shown in a cutaway view in the lower neck region. The hyoid bone is positioned below the platysma muscle, in the midline of the neck. The fascia of the infrahyoid muscles is highlighted in the lower neck, pointing to the muscles beneath the hyoid bone. The superficial cervical fascia is shown in a cutaway view, pointing to the superficial layer of the neck. The cricoid cartilage is positioned below the thyroid cartilage, in the lower part of the larynx. The suprasternal space, also known as the space of Burns, is indicated above the manubrium of the sternum. The manubrium itself is located at the top of the sternum, pointing to the upper part of the breastbone. The jugular notch is positioned at the top of the sternum, between the clavicular heads. Additionally, the digastric muscle's anterior belly, mylohyoid muscle, and submandibular gland are labeled, with the digastric muscle pointing to its anterior part, the mylohyoid muscle below the mandible, and the submandibular gland also below the mandible. The fibrous loop for the intermediate tendon is partially labeled." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_neck/s4> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/assert/21d0c576002b> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#polarity> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#Uncertain> <urn:graph:anatomy_of_neck/s4> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/assert/9950d5384860> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#assertionType> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#located_in> <urn:graph:anatomy_of_neck/s4> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p3_b8> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The image presents a close-up of a person's neck from the side, with the smooth skin and natural texture prominently displayed. The thyroid cartilage is labeled near the upper part of the neck, just below the chin, while the cricoid cartilage is labeled lower down, just above the Adam's apple. A red border frames the image, and red lines appear at the top right corner. The background is dark, providing a contrast to the lighter skin tone of the neck." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_neck/s4> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/c73352df1a42> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#object> <http://snomed.info/id/353295004> <urn:graph:anatomy_of_neck/s4> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/assert/7d58b15c7e1a> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#polarity> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#Uncertain> <urn:graph:anatomy_of_neck/s4> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p5_b3> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The image presents a side view of a person's neck, with a dark background that makes the neck and labels stand out. The labels are written in black text on white lines pointing to specific parts of the neck. The angle of mandible is labeled near the lower jaw, while the mental protuberance is indicated at the chin area. The body of hyoid is marked at the base of the tongue, and the laryngeal prominence, or Adam's apple, is labeled as the visible protrusion of the thyroid cartilage. Just below this, the cricothyroid membrane is labeled, followed by the cricoid cartilage, which is situated below the membrane. The person's ear and part of the cheek are visible, but no other body parts are shown." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_neck/s4> .
