<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p20_b6> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The external oblique muscle is positioned on the outer side of the abdomen and is shown in orange, appearing on both sides of the body. The rectus abdominis muscle is centrally located in the abdomen, depicted in a lighter blue, running vertically from the lower back to the pubic area. Behind the rectus abdominis, the posterior wall of the rectus sheath is shown in light blue. The internal oblique muscle is situated on the inner side of the abdomen, also in orange, and positioned on both sides. The arcuate line, a curved feature, is located at the lower edge of the internal oblique muscle and is shown in light blue. Beneath the internal oblique, the transversalis fascia is depicted in green. At the base of the rectus abdominis, the pyramidalis muscle is shown in light blue at the bottom center of the image. Each structure is labeled with a leader line or arrow pointing to it, with labels placed to the left of the image." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s3> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p19_b7> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#caption> "The figure illustrates the detailed anatomy of the abdominal muscles and related structures, including the external and internal oblique muscles, rectus abdominis, and associated fascia and lines." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s3> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p19_b7> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/244944008> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s3> .
