<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p18_b6> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The illustration presents a detailed view of the abdominal muscles and surrounding structures. The external oblique muscle is labeled on both sides of the abdomen, indicating its position as the outermost layer of the abdominal wall. A label points to the tenth rib, referred to as Rib X, in the ribcage area. The transversus abdominis muscle and its aponeurosis are labeled, highlighting their location in the deeper layer of the abdominal wall. The aponeurosis of the external oblique is shown as the connective tissue forming the outer layer, while the aponeurosis of the internal oblique is depicted as the inner layer. The anterior superior iliac spine is labeled at the front of the pelvis, and the linea alba is marked at the midline of the abdomen. An inset diagram at the bottom provides a close-up view of the transversus abdominis muscle, labeled \"TA,\" and its relationship with other structures, including a portion of the pelvic bone labeled \"ID.\" The overall layout is a sagittal view, with the linea alba running vertically down the center." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s3> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p18_b6> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#caption> "The figure illustrates the anatomy of the abdominal wall, including the external oblique muscle, rib X, transversus abdominis muscle, and other key structures." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s3> .
