<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p21_b9> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The illustration presents an anterior view of the human abdomen, with the abdominal muscles and surrounding structures clearly labeled. The linea alba is depicted as the midline where the rectus abdominis muscles meet. The tendinous intersections are shown as horizontal lines within the rectus abdominis. The aponeurosis of the internal oblique and the arcuate line are also visible, marking the transition between the external and internal oblique muscles. The pyramidalis muscle is shown as a small triangle below the rectus abdominis, and the cremasteric muscle is positioned near the inguinal ligament. The costal margin and the tenth rib are indicated at the upper part of the illustration. The semicircular line marks the boundary of the rectus abdominis muscle, and the posterior wall of the rectus sheath is visible." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s3> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/292ed41dfdf9> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#object> <http://snomed.info/id/162239000> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s3> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/292ed41dfdf9> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#Statement> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s3> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/assert/814a5032aa92> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#polarity> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#Uncertain> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s3> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p19_b7> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/31243008> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s3> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p14_b10> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The illustration features a detailed view of the human torso and lower abdomen, with the skeletal structure visible, including the ribcage, spine, pelvis, and femur bones. Muscles are color-coded and labeled with black text connected by black lines or arrows. The external oblique muscle is shown in orange on the outer side of the abdominal wall, while the internal oblique, also in orange, is positioned deeper and on the inner side. The transversus abdominis is depicted in a darker shade of orange, located deep within the abdominal wall. The rectus abdominis is shown in a lighter shade of orange, running vertically along the front of the abdomen. The quadratus lumborum, in a darker shade of orange, is located in the lower back area. The iliacus and psoas major muscles are both shown in light blue near the hip joint. A label points to the gap between the inguinal ligament and the pelvic bone, highlighting a gap in the abdominal wall." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s3> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/3ddf11874668> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#object> <http://snomed.info/id/37198003> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s3> .
