<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p36_b7> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The diagram is divided into two main sections: the lumen of the intestine at the top and the intestinal mucosa with a capillary at the bottom. The lumen is labeled at the top left, while the intestinal mucosa is indicated on the left side, and the capillary is labeled at the bottom center. A red dashed box over the lumen contains text explaining that glucose enters with sodium ions on SGLT and exits on GLUT2. A blue dashed box to the right explains that fructose enters on GLUT5 and exits on GLUT2. Arrows and lines connect these elements, showing the transport direction from the lumen through the intestinal mucosa to the capillary. The lumen connects to the intestinal mucosa with dashed lines, and solid arrows indicate the movement of substances into the capillary. Different colors are used: blue circles for sodium ions, purple hexagons for glucose or galactose, and pink pentagons for fructose. The intestinal mucosa is light blue with wavy lines, and the capillary is a red ring." <urn:graph:digestive_system/s4> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p35_b2> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://purl.bioontology.org/ontology/RXNORM/1540> <urn:graph:digestive_system/s4> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/f20263714716> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#subject> <http://snomed.info/id/1732005> <urn:graph:digestive_system/s4> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p35_b2> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The diagram is divided into three main sections. On the left, green dots labeled \"Bile salts\" are positioned at the top, with yellow circles labeled \"Large fat droplets from stomach\" below them. These elements interact to form \"Emulsion,\" represented by yellow circles, which then break down further into \"Micelles,\" also depicted as yellow circles. In the middle section, the area labeled as the \"Lumen of Small Intestine\" shows the process where \"Pancreatic lipase and colipase break down fats into monoglycerides and fatty acids stored in micelles.\" The movement of \"Monoglycerides and Fatty Acids\" out of the micelles is illustrated, along with \"Cholesterol\" being transported into cells. Absorbed fats combine with cholesterol and proteins to form \"Chylomicrons,\" shown as yellow spheres. On the right side, the smooth endoplasmic reticulum and the Golgi apparatus are depicted within the cells. Chylomicrons are shown forming and moving towards the interstitial fluid, which is represented as a blue region. A red capillary is positioned on the right, and a green lacteal collects the chylomicrons, indicating the pathway to the lymph and eventually the vena cava." <urn:graph:digestive_system/s4> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p38_b7> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://purl.bioontology.org/ontology/RXNORM/318> <urn:graph:digestive_system/s4> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p38_b7> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/53982002> <urn:graph:digestive_system/s4> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/68c30253a8fc> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#predicate> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#requires> <urn:graph:digestive_system/s4> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/assert/cac32bea243e> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#target> <http://snomed.info/id/17534000> <urn:graph:digestive_system/s4> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p38_b7> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The diagram is divided into a light blue region for the cell interior and a darker blue region for the extracellular space, with a dashed line indicating calcium movement between them. At the top right, a white box states \"Paracellular absorption is not regulated,\" while another box below the dashed line says \"Transcellular transport is regulated by vitamin D₃.\" Arrows labeled \"Ca²⁺\" show calcium ions entering from the extracellular space into the cell and moving inside the cell, with a dotted arrow indicating calcium exiting back to the extracellular space. A dotted arrow labeled \"3 Na⁺\" shows sodium ions entering the cell. A curved arrow labeled \"Ca²⁺\" points from the cell interior to an oval labeled \"ATP,\" signifying ATP's role. Calcium ions are represented by small circles with plus signs, sodium ions by pink circles, and ATP by a blue oval. A curved line labeled \"Ca channel\" points to a specific area within the double-layered cell membrane." <urn:graph:digestive_system/s4> .
