<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p39_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "At the top of the image, the hypothalamus and anterior pituitary are depicted with arrows pointing to each, indicating their roles in hormone secretion and release. A central box labeled \"Growth hormone (GH)\" is connected by arrows to various sections of the flowchart. An arrow from the GH box leads to \"Liver and other tissues,\" which in turn leads to \"Insulin-like growth factors (IGFs).\" Two arrows from IGFs point to \"Skeletal\" and \"Extraskeletal\" effects, showing increased cartilage formation, skeletal growth, protein synthesis, and cell growth. Another set of arrows from the GH box directly points to \"Fat metabolism\" and \"Carbohydrate metabolism,\" indicating increased fat breakdown and anti-insulin effects. On the left side, a dotted arrow labeled \"Feedback\" points towards the hypothalamus, showing the inhibition of GHRH release, stimulation of GHIH release, and inhibition of GH synthesis and release. The legend at the bottom right explains that solid arrows signify increases or stimulation, dotted arrows signify reductions or inhibition, pink boxes are initial stimuli, and green boxes are physiological responses." <urn:graph:endocrine/s9> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p38_b2> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "A red line on the graph fluctuates throughout the day, showing peaks and troughs. The x-axis is labeled with time intervals from 8 am to 8 am, marked at 4-hour intervals, and the y-axis is labeled \"Growth hormone (ng/ml plasma)\" with a range from 0 to 30 ng/ml. The graph is titled \"Sleep\" at the top right corner. Two distinct peaks are labeled \"Strenuous exercise,\" occurring around noon and midnight." <urn:graph:endocrine/s9> .
