<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p58_b2> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/69695003> <urn:graph:anatomy_of_endocrine_glands/s20> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p59_b7> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The image presents a detailed anatomical illustration of the human abdominal cavity. At the top, an arrow points to the diaphragm. To the right of the diaphragm, an arrow indicates the liver. The lesser omentum is highlighted in yellow, appearing as a fold of tissue connecting the stomach to the liver. Below the liver, an arrow points to the stomach. The transverse mesocolon is labeled with an arrow, showing its connection to the posterior abdominal wall. An arrow in the mid-abdomen points to the transverse colon. The greater sac is indicated by an arrow pointing to the large space within the abdominal cavity. The greater omentum is also highlighted in yellow, depicted as a fold of tissue extending from the greater curvature of the stomach to the transverse colon. The small intestine, shown as a coiled structure, is labeled in the lower part of the abdomen. Near the lesser omentum, an arrow points to the omental bursa. The pancreas is labeled with an arrow pointing to its position behind the stomach. The duodenum, the first part of the small intestine, is labeled with an arrow. The mesentery is labeled with an arrow, shown as a fold of tissue suspending the small intestine from the posterior abdominal wall. On the right side, the spine is labeled, though it does not have an arrow pointing to it." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_endocrine_glands/s20> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p58_b2> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/38848004> <urn:graph:anatomy_of_endocrine_glands/s20> .
