<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p90_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The figure is divided into two main sections. On the left, there's a simplified diagram of the human torso featuring internal organs. The liver is colored blue and positioned at the upper left, extending into the chest area. The stomach is shown in green, and the intestines are in pink, both occupying the lower abdomen and extending towards the pelvis. On the right, a simplified diagram of the human torso without internal organs is displayed. Three colored circles are placed on the abdomen: a blue circle at the top labeled \"Foregut – pain localises to epigastric area,\" an orange circle in the middle labeled \"Midgut – pain localises to periumbilical area,\" and a green circle at the bottom labeled \"Hindgut – pain localises to suprapubic area.\" A legend on the rightmost section explains the color coding: blue for foregut, orange for midgut, and green for hindgut." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s1> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p88_b3> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The image presents a human torso with sections demarcated by dashed black lines. A vertical midclavicular line splits the chest into left and right halves, while a horizontal subcostal line divides the abdomen just below the rib cage. Another horizontal transtubercular line crosses the pelvis above the iliac crests. The torso is segmented into regions marked with different colors: red, yellow, green, blue, gray, and orange. Specific areas are labeled with arrows pointing to the right upper quadrant (RH), epigastric region (E), left upper quadrant (LH), right lower quadrant (RL), umbilical region (U), left lower quadrant (LL), right iliac region (RI), pubic region (P), and left iliac region (LI)." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s1> .
