<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p89_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The diagram presents a view of the human torso with a vertical line running from the top of the head down the center, dividing the body into left and right halves. The diaphragm is positioned at the top, above the liver and the lungs, indicating its role in separating the thoracic and abdominal cavities. The liver is shown on the right side of the upper abdomen, just beneath the diaphragm, with the gallbladder situated slightly lower and to the right. The stomach is located centrally in the upper abdomen, below the diaphragm and above the intestines. The spleen is positioned on the left side of the upper abdomen, also below the diaphragm. The quadrants of the abdomen are demarcated by the transumbilical plane, which runs horizontally across the diagram, dividing the abdomen into upper and lower sections. The ascending colon is depicted on the right side of the abdomen, below the liver and gallbladder, while the descending colon is on the left side, below the spleen. The sigmoid colon is shown in the lower left part of the abdomen." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s1> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p89_b1> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#FigureImage> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s1> .
