<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p70_b4> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The diagram is organized with the spinal cord positioned on the left side of the image, depicted as a cross-section featuring a central canal and outer layers. Extending vertically from the spinal cord is a series of yellowish structures, representing the sympathetic chain ganglion, located towards the top right of the image. A red line, labeled as the spinal nerve, emerges from the spinal cord and connects to the sympathetic chain ganglion. Another red line, labeled as the splanchnic nerve, branches off from the spinal nerve and continues downward to connect with the prevertebral ganglion, a yellowish structure situated below the sympathetic chain ganglion. Red lines then extend from the prevertebral ganglion to the adrenal medulla, which is illustrated in a separate section on the right side of the image. The adrenal medulla is depicted as a small, irregularly shaped structure containing small red dots, indicating axon terminals. Text labels in the diagram include \"No synapse in chain ganglion\" near the spinal nerve and sympathetic chain ganglion, \"No synapse in prevertebral ganglion\" near the prevertebral ganglion, and \"Axons terminate in adrenal medulla\" near the adrenal medulla." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_endocrine_glands/s17> .
