<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p25_b8> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/255561001> <urn:graph:anatomy_of_endocrine_glands/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p21_b10> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The figure presents a detailed anatomical illustration of the pituitary gland and its surrounding structures. In the top left section, a small inset image provides a cross-sectional view of the brain, with the hypothalamus and pituitary gland highlighted. The hypothalamus is marked by an arrow, and the pituitary gland is depicted in purple within the sella turcica of the sphenoid bone. The main section of the image offers a larger, more detailed view of the pituitary gland and its connections. The hypothalamus is labeled at the top, the mammillary body is on the right, the median eminence is in the center, the optic chiasm is below the median eminence, and the infundibulum is centrally located, connecting the hypothalamus to the pituitary gland. The anterior pituitary is divided into three parts: the pars tuberalis on the left, the pars intermedia in the middle, and the pars distalis on the right. The posterior pituitary is labeled on the right, with the infundibular stalk connecting the hypothalamus to it and the pars nervosa at the lower part. At the bottom, the hypophyseal fossa in the sella turcica of the sphenoid bone is labeled, indicating the hollow space where the pituitary gland is situated. Arrows and lines connect the labels to specific parts of the pituitary gland and surrounding structures, providing a clear layout of the anatomical features." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_endocrine_glands/s5> .
<http://snomed.info/id/399244003> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#caused_by> <http://snomed.info/id/272676008> <urn:graph:anatomy_of_endocrine_glands/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p29_b6> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The illustration presents a detailed view of the pituitary gland with several labeled pathways and arteries. At the top left, the supraoptico-hypophyseal tract is shown, extending from the hypothalamus to the posterior lobe of the pituitary gland. Near the top center, the paraventriculo-hypophyseal tract is positioned, also pointing to a pathway from the hypothalamus to the posterior lobe. The tubero-hypophyseal tract is located at the top right, indicating a connection from the hypothalamus to the anterior lobe. On the left side, the anterior branch of the hypophyseal artery is depicted, supplying blood to the anterior lobe, while the superior hypophyseal artery, a branch of the intracranial portion of the internal carotid artery, is also shown on the left, supplying blood to the superior part of the pituitary gland. The optic chiasm is situated at the top left, marking the crossing point of the optic nerves. Centrally, the gomitoli is shown as a network of blood vessels within the pituitary gland. Near the center, the trabecular artery, also known as the loral artery, is depicted as a small artery branching off from the main artery supply. The pars tuberalis is positioned near the center, representing a thin layer of tissue surrounding the stalk. Long portal vessels are shown near the center, forming a network of blood vessels connecting the hypothalamus to the anterior lobe." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_endocrine_glands/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/00d45101e182> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#Statement> <urn:graph:anatomy_of_endocrine_glands/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/00d45101e182> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#object> <http://snomed.info/id/272676008> <urn:graph:anatomy_of_endocrine_glands/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p25_b8> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#caption> "The figure illustrates the anterior surface of the sphenoid bone, highlighting key anatomical features such as the superior orbital fissure, sphenoidal sinus, lesser wing, orbital surface of the greater wing, pterygoid canal, pterygoid process, medial plate, and lateral plate." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_endocrine_glands/s5> .
