<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p83_b2> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The diagram presents a detailed cross-section of a liver lobule, with the hepatic portal vein depicted as a blue vessel at the top left, entering the liver. Near it, the hepatic artery is shown as a red vessel branching off from the portal vein. The biliary duct is represented by a green duct, also branching off from the portal vein. Kupffer cells, labeled with arrows, are situated near the sinusoids, appearing yellow. Bile canaliculi are indicated by green arrows pointing downward, showing the direction of bile flow from hepatocytes. The perisinusoidal spaces are illustrated as spaces surrounding the sinusoids, while the peribiliary arterial plexus is a network of small blood vessels around the biliary ducts. The central vein is a blue vessel at the bottom right, collecting blood from the sinusoids. Hepatocytes, the primary cells of the liver lobule, are shown in red. The deep lymphatic duct is a green duct on the left, pointing to the perisinusoidal space. Blood flow in the sinusoids is indicated by green arrows, showing the direction from the interlobular artery and portal vein. The entire structure of the liver lobule is labeled, along with the interlobular portal triads." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s20> .
