<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p61_b8> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The illustration presents the large intestine in a relaxed state, with natural folds and curves. The right colic flexure is positioned at the top left, directing attention to the right side of the ascending colon. The transverse colon stretches horizontally across the middle, moving from right to left. The left colic flexure is situated at the top right, pointing towards the left side of the descending colon. The ascending colon runs vertically on the left, extending upwards. Near the top right of the ascending colon, the omental appendage appears as a small, curved structure. The haustra of the colon are visible as pouch-like structures along the descending colon, indicated by a label in the lower right section. The taeniae coli is a band-like structure running along the left side of the ascending colon. The ileum, a part of the small intestine, connects to the cecum, which is located below the ascending colon. The appendix, a small, finger-like structure, extends from the cecum, positioned below and slightly to the right. The sigmoid colon curves downward from the descending colon, situated towards the bottom right. The rectum is a straight, tube-like structure extending downward from the sigmoid colon, located at the bottom right. Finally, the anal canal is a short, narrow tube extending downward from the rectum, placed at the very bottom right of the image. Each label points directly to its corresponding part of the intestine." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s15> .
