<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p65_b7> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The illustration presents a transverse section of the neck at the level of C6, with a superior view. Various structures and layers are labeled, and color-coded regions are used to distinguish different tissue layers." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_neck/s11> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p69_b2> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The image is oriented vertically, with the spinal column running up the center. The vertebrae, labeled from C1 to T1, are depicted with their characteristic shapes, including the vertebral body and spinous process. The vertebral artery runs along the side of the spinal column, while the subclavian artery is shown at the base of the neck, branching off from the vertebral artery. The ansa subclavia appears as a looped structure near the subclavian artery. Nerves and ganglia are highlighted in yellow, while the arteries are depicted in red. The superior cervical sympathetic ganglion is positioned at the top of the image, connected to the cervical sympathetic trunk, which runs alongside the vertebral artery. The middle cervical ganglion is located below the superior cervical ganglion, and the vertebral ganglion is near the vertebral artery. The inferior cervical ganglion (cervicothoracic stellate) is shown at the base of the neck, near the subclavian artery, with the first thoracic sympathetic ganglion labeled at the bottom. Each structure is labeled with text to the left, connected by arrows or lines to the corresponding areas on the right side of the image." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_neck/s11> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/assert/94e9a818fda6> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#about> <http://snomed.info/id/118600007> <urn:graph:anatomy_of_neck/s11> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p10_b6> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#caption> "The figure illustrates the cervical spine, focusing on the axis (C2 vertebra), atlas (C1 vertebra), and the atlanto-occipital joint, with labeled anatomical features." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_neck/s11> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p52_b8> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The submandibular triangle is positioned at the bottom left of the image, highlighted in green, with an arrow pointing to it. The anterior belly of the digastric muscle is shown in pinkish-red within the triangle, indicated by an arrow. The posterior belly of the digastric muscle is on the right side, extending vertically downward in darker gray, with an arrow pointing to it. The mandible is at the top right, depicted in light gray, and an arrow directs to it." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_neck/s11> .
