<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p49_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The outer ear, or pinna, is positioned at the top left of the image, appearing as a pinkish-red, fleshy structure with a slightly curved shape. Extending from the outer ear is the external auditory canal, a narrow tube-like structure shown in a darker red color. At the end of this canal lies the tympanic membrane, or eardrum, a thin, circular membrane centrally positioned within the middle ear cavity. The middle ear cavity itself is a space behind the tympanic membrane, containing the ossicles—three small bones colored light yellowish: the malleus at the front, the incus in the middle, and the stapes at the back. On the right side of the image, near the bottom, the Eustachian tube is depicted as a small, curved tube extending from the middle ear cavity to the nasopharynx. Various muscles and soft tissues are illustrated around the ear and middle ear area in shades of pink and red, with some areas highlighted in lighter colors. The background is white, enhancing the visibility of the anatomical structures. Labels point to specific parts of the ear, such as the middle ear cavity and the ossicles, though no text is provided in the image itself." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_face_and_oral_cavity/s9> .
