<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p26_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The illustration presents a detailed cross-section of the brain, focusing on the hypothalamus and pituitary gland. The optic chiasm is positioned at the top left, directing towards the center. Above the hypothalamus lies the third ventricle, pointing centrally. To the right of the hypothalamus is the median eminence, a small, rounded structure. The infundibulum, a thin, elongated structure, connects the hypothalamus downward to the pituitary gland. Beneath the infundibulum, the diaphragma sellae is a thin, circular structure. The pars tuberalis is situated on the lower part of the hypothalamus, pointing left. The posterior pituitary (pars nervosa) is a small, rounded structure on the lower part of the pituitary gland, pointing right. The sphenoid bone (sella turcica), a large, irregularly shaped structure, surrounds the pituitary gland at the bottom, providing structural support. The pars distalis, the largest part of the anterior pituitary gland, occupies the lower portion of the pituitary gland, pointing right." <urn:graph:endocrine/s5> .
