<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p58_b2> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The diagram is organized to show the flow of hormones from the hypothalamus to the posterior pituitary. At the top-left, a section of the brain is depicted with the \"Posterior lobe of pituitary\" highlighted and an arrow pointing towards the right. In the central region, the hypothalamus is prominently displayed at the top-center, with the \"Paraventricular nucleus\" on the left and the \"Supraoptic nucleus\" on the right. Yellow lines represent axons connecting to the posterior pituitary, while the optic chiasm is shown below the hypothalamus. The infundibulum, or connecting stalk, is labeled, linking the hypothalamus to the posterior pituitary. The inferior hypophyseal artery is shown as a red line extending from the hypothalamus to the posterior pituitary. On the right side, four numbered steps in blue text explain the hormone release process. At the bottom, the posterior lobe of the pituitary is shown with blue lines indicating axon terminals where oxytocin and ADH are stored, and the text \"Oxytocin ADH\" is written nearby. Arrows and leader lines connect the labeled parts of the brain and pituitary to the corresponding structures in the text." <urn:graph:endocrine/s5> .
