<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p28_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The image displays a histological section of the pituitary gland, with the three main parts labeled: Pars distalis, Pars intermedia, and Pars nervosa. Sinusoidal capillaries appear as irregularly shaped, thin-walled blood vessels with a pinkish hue in the Pars distalis. Chromophobe cells, with their pale cytoplasm, are scattered throughout the Pars distalis and Pars intermedia. Acidophils, or alpha cells, are found in the Pars distalis and have a more intensely stained, darker pink cytoplasm compared to chromophobe cells. The nuclei of pituicytes are elongated and aligned in the Pars nervosa. Follicles in the Pars intermedia are oval-shaped with a central lumen filled with a pinkish substance." <urn:graph:histology_endocrine/s3> .
