<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p55_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The image is divided into four quadrants labeled A through D. In Quadrant A, representing a healthy patient, a cross-sectional view of the abdominal area is shown. The left renal vein is labeled with an arrow pointing to the upper left side, while the superior mesenteric artery is labeled with an arrow pointing to the lower left side. The duodenum is labeled at the bottom, pointing to the central area, and the aorta is shown on the right side. Quadrant B, depicting the Nutcracker Phenomenon, mirrors the layout of Quadrant A with the same labels and arrow directions. Quadrant C, illustrating Wilkie Syndrome, shows a diagram of the stomach and surrounding structures. The abdominal aorta is labeled with an arrow pointing to the left side, the stomach is centered, the duodenum is labeled with an arrow pointing to the lower left side, and the superior mesenteric artery is similarly labeled. Quadrant D, focusing on Nutcracker Syndrome, presents a detailed diagram of the abdominal vasculature. The left renal vein is labeled with an arrow pointing to the upper left side, the cava vein is labeled with an arrow also pointing to the upper left side, the superior mesenteric artery is labeled with an arrow pointing to the lower left side, the left kidney is shown on the right side, the gonadal vein is labeled with an arrow pointing to the lower right side, and the aorta is shown on the right side." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s19> .
