<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p73_b5> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The illustration presents a detailed view of the abdominal vasculature, with the left gastric artery positioned at the top left, pointing towards the left side of the stomach. The splenic artery is situated above the pancreas, directing towards the left side of the pancreas. Near the middle of the pancreas, the greater pancreatic artery points towards the dorsal aspect. Below the celiac trunk, the common hepatic artery is positioned, pointing to the right side of the pancreas. The gastroduodenal artery is situated below the common hepatic artery, also pointing to the right side. At the bottom of the pancreas, the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery directs to the inferior aspect. The superior mesenteric artery is positioned at the bottom right of the image, not directly connected to the pancreas but included in the diagram. The pancreas is depicted in a cross-sectional view, showing its internal structure and the arteries supplying it, with the right hypochondrium and epigastric region extending into the left hypochondrium." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s19> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p79_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The diagram is a sagittal section, meaning it cuts through the body from side to side, revealing the internal organs and their relationships. The diaphragm is labeled at the top left, pointing to the uppermost part of the image. The lung is located above the liver, indicated by a label pointing to the upper right area. The anterior layer of the coronary ligament is labeled between the diaphragm and the liver. The bare area of the liver is labeled in the central part of the liver, where there is no covering tissue. The liver itself is the main organ depicted in the center of the image, with two distinct surfaces highlighted. The posterior layer of the coronary ligament is labeled behind the liver. The posterior abdominal muscles are labeled in the lower part of the image. The right kidney is labeled in the lower right area. At the bottom left, a legend describes the liver's surface: the diaphragmatic surface is shown in purple at the top of the liver, and the visceral surface is shown in orange at the bottom." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s19> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p53_b6> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/399033003> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s19> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/943b2e603241> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#object> <http://snomed.info/id/31243008> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s19> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/4efca170e965> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#subject> <http://snomed.info/id/699638000> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s19> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p73_b5> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/15776009> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s19> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p55_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/272615000> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s19> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p54_b7> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The illustration presents a detailed view of the abdominal vasculature, with the posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery branching off from the superior mesenteric artery at the top of the pancreas. The left gastric artery is positioned on the left side, extending towards the stomach, while the right gastro-omental artery is located on the right side, also near the stomach. The hepatic artery proper is situated just below the posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery, branching off from the celiac trunk. The supraduodenal artery is positioned above the gastroduodenal artery, extending towards the duodenum. The posterior inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery branches off from the superior mesenteric artery and is located at the bottom of the pancreas. The abdominal aorta is depicted at the bottom of the image, with branches extending upwards, and the superior mesenteric artery branches off from the abdominal aorta and is located centrally in the lower part of the image. The pancreas and duodenum are shown in semi-transparent manner, allowing the underlying vasculature and their relationships to be clearly seen." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s19> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p24_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The image shows a table layout with five rows and five columns." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s19> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p79_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/10200004> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s19> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/assert/ab6db76cdaa1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#assertionType> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#part_of> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s19> .
