<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p62_b9> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The image displays a detailed anatomical illustration of the posterior neck muscles. The spine and surrounding structures are depicted in a semi-transparent manner, allowing visibility of the muscles. The muscles are colored in red, while the bones and other structures are in a lighter color, providing contrast for clarity. The overall layout shows the muscles in relation to the cervical vertebrae and the skull. The Rectus capitis posterior minor is positioned at the top left of the image, with a leader line pointing to the uppermost part of the neck, just below the skull base. The Rectus capitis posterior major is situated slightly below the Rectus capitis posterior minor, with a leader line pointing to the middle section of the neck, just above the cervical vertebrae. The Obliquus capitis superior is located on the right side of the image, with a leader line pointing to the upper right side of the neck, near the skull base. The Obliquus capitis inferior is positioned on the right side of the image, below the Obliquus capitis superior, with a leader line pointing to the lower right side of the neck, closer to the cervical vertebrae." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_neck/s1> .
