<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p61_b8> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The illustration presents a detailed view of the neck's anatomy, with the anterior scalene muscle positioned on the left side, colored red and extending from the first rib to the base of the skull. On the right side, the medius scalene muscle, also in red, stretches from the second rib to the skull base, alongside the posterior scalene muscle, which is similarly colored and extends from the third rib. Central to the image are the cervical vertebrae, numbered 1st, 2nd, 3rd, 6th, and 7th, depicted in black and white with clear outlines of the spinous processes and laminae. Various muscles and ligaments are labeled in black text above their corresponding structures, including the longus capitis, transverse process, atlas, occipital condyle, and scalenus medius. At the top, the skull is shown, featuring the occipital bone, atlas, and other cranial bones, while the ribs are labeled as the 1st and 2nd at the bottom of the image." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_neck/s1> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/4f625ae8b7b5> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#predicate> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#part_of> <urn:graph:anatomy_of_neck/s1> .
