<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p60_b11> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The illustration presents a detailed view of the neck anatomy, with the basilar part of the occipital bone situated at the top, forming the base of the skull. Near this, the occipital condyle is visible as a bony prominence. The transverse processes of the atlas (C1) and axis (C2) vertebrae are depicted as small projections, while the anterior and posterior tubercles of the C3 vertebra are shown as smaller projections. The posterior tubercle of the C7 vertebra is also visible. Muscles are intricately illustrated, with the longus capitis and rectus capitis anterior muscles running along the back and front of the neck, respectively. The anterior, middle, and posterior scalene muscles are depicted running along the side of the neck, with the origins of the anterior and posterior scalene muscles shown, the latter partially cut away to reveal internal structures." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_neck/s1> .
