<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p84_b5> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The diagram is organized with the left and right hepatic ducts positioned at the top, merging to form the common hepatic duct. Below these, the common hepatic duct is shown joining with the cystic duct, which is situated near the gallbladder, to form the common bile duct. The gallbladder itself is depicted on the left side of the image, indicating its role in storing bile. Central to the diagram is the hepatopancreatic ampulla, where the common bile duct and the main pancreatic duct converge, regulated by the hepatopancreatic sphincter. The major duodenal papilla is positioned at the lower end of the hepatopancreatic ampulla, marking the primary entry point for bile and pancreatic juices into the duodenum. The common bile duct is shown extending from the hepatopancreatic ampulla into the duodenum, while the accessory pancreatic duct, also known as the Santorini duct, runs parallel and empties into the duodenum separately. The main pancreatic duct is depicted merging with the common bile duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla, carrying pancreatic juice into the duodenum. Arrows or leader lines connect these structures, illustrating the sequential flow of bile and pancreatic juices." <urn:graph:histology_digestive_1/s8> .
