<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p71_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The image presents a detailed microscopic view of tissue, likely from an exocrine gland, stained with hematoxylin and eosin (H&E). At the top left, venules are visible as small blood vessels with thin walls and lumen, adjacent to the interlobular excretory duct. Adipose cells, appearing as pale, round structures, are scattered throughout the tissue, particularly in the upper middle section. Serous acini, clusters of glandular units that secrete serous fluid, are primarily situated in the lower middle and right sections. Interlobular connective tissue septa, fibrous partitions between lobules, are seen in the lower middle section. Myoepithelial cells, specialized cells with contractile properties, are located at the bottom left surrounding the serous acinus. Another set of serous acini is shown in more detail in the bottom left corner. Near the top right, an arteriole, a small artery with a thicker wall compared to the venules, is visible. Intercalated ducts, short and straight ducts, are shown in the central and right sections, while striated ducts, larger ducts with striated muscle layers, are located on the right side. Intercalated ducts are also shown in more detail in the bottom right corner. Interlobular excretory ducts, larger ducts connecting the acini to the interlobular ducts, are located at the bottom right. The image includes four magnified insets labeled I through IV, providing close-ups of the serous acinus, intercalated duct, striated duct, and interlobular excretory duct, respectively, highlighting their cellular and structural details." <urn:graph:histology_digestive_1/s8> .
