<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p105_b6> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The diagram is a detailed anatomical illustration of a hepatic lobule, with the central vein positioned at the top and the sublobular vein at the bottom. The periportal space is situated at the periphery, marked by a blue cylindrical structure on the left. A red vessel represents the terminal branch of the hepatic artery, while a blue vessel indicates the terminal branch of the portal vein. These vessels are connected by a green structure known as the portal canal. Within the lobule, a small circular structure represents a capillary, surrounded by a network of thin interconnected vessels, the hepatic sinusoids. Blue inlet venules and a red arteriosinusoidal branch feed into these sinusoids. A green bile duct exits the lobule, and a yellow-green lymphatic vessel is also present." <urn:graph:histology_digestive_1/s7> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p102_b4> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The image displays a histological section stained with hematoxylin and eosin (H&E). A dense cellular area is visible, with a central circular region highlighted by a circular marking. Within this central region, several small green dots are scattered throughout. The background consists of a pinkish-red tissue matrix, featuring numerous small, dark nuclei distributed throughout, indicating a cellular environment. In the bottom right corner, a blue box with white text that reads \"KEN HUB\" is present, likely indicating the source or provider of the image." <urn:graph:histology_digestive_1/s7> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p91_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/256002> <urn:graph:histology_digestive_1/s7> .
