<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p94_b2> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The diagram presents a cross-sectional view of a liver lobule, with the portal triad situated at the bottom. The hepatic artery is depicted as a red tube-like structure on the right side of the portal triad, while the portal vein is shown as a blue tube-like structure on the left. The bile duct, represented in green, is positioned between the hepatic artery and the portal vein. Arrows indicate blood flow entering the liver through both the hepatic artery and portal vein, while bile flow is shown entering and exiting through the bile duct. The central vein is a dark blue structure located at the center of the liver lobule, with arrows showing blood flow from the liver tissue into it. The liver parenchyma is depicted in shades of pink and green, containing the central vein and Kupffer cells, which appear as small pinkish structures near the central vein. Labels such as \"blood flow,\" \"bile flow,\" \"Kupffer cell,\" \"bile duct,\" \"portal vein,\" \"hepatic artery,\" and \"central vein\" are placed around the diagram with arrows pointing to the respective structures." <urn:graph:histology_digestive_1/s7> .
