<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p103_b2> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#caption> "The figure illustrates the detailed structure of a liver cell, focusing on the sinusoidal region, including components like the endothelial cell, smooth endoplasmic reticulum, collagen fibers, Kupffer cell, lumen of the hepatic sinusoid, Golgi apparatus, lipid droplets, Ito cell, mitochondria, glycogen, bile canaliculus, zonula occludens, and space of Disse." <urn:graph:histology_digestive_1/s7> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p108_b6> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The diagram presents two hexagonal liver lobules side by side. Each lobule features a central vein at its core, depicted as a blue circle with a smaller blue circle inside. The lobule is divided into three color-coded zones: Zone 1 in purple, closest to the central vein; Zone 2 in green, the middle area; and Zone 3 in yellow, the outermost area. Arrows mark the long axis from top to bottom and the short axis from left to right. The term \"triad\" is positioned on the right, pointing to where the central vein branches. The lobule is filled with a red pattern, likely representing blood vessels, and small colored dots (blue, red, and green) are scattered throughout, possibly indicating different cells or structures. A thin blue line connects the lobules, delineating their boundary." <urn:graph:histology_digestive_1/s7> .
