<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p30_b5> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The diagram places the thyroid gland at the top, colored light purple, above the trachea. The inferior thyroid artery is depicted as a red vessel branching off from the descending thoracic aorta and pointing downward from the thyroid gland. The thyrocerical trunk is shown as a red vessel branching upward and slightly to the right from the subclavian artery. The subclavian artery itself is a large blue vessel on the right side, pointing downward and to the left. The descending thoracic aorta is a large blue vessel at the bottom right, pointing upward and to the left. The upper and lower left bronchial arteries, as well as the 3rd right posterior intercostal artery, are all red vessels branching off the descending thoracic aorta, with the upper left pointing downward and to the left, the lower left pointing downward and to the right, and the 3rd right posterior intercostal artery also pointing downward and to the left. The right bronchial artery, another red vessel, branches off from the 3rd right posterior intercostal artery and points downward and to the left. Each structure is labeled in black text and connected by a leader line or arrow." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_neck/s9> .
