<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p42_b6> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "A large red vessel, the aorta, runs vertically through the center of the image, branching into several arteries such as the brachiocephalic trunk, common carotid artery, subclavian artery, and internal thoracic artery. Two blue circles highlight specific branches of the aorta that supply the esophagus: one labeled \"Esophageal branch of inferior thyroid artery\" and another labeled \"Esophageal branches of thoracic aorta.\" The trachea runs parallel to the aorta, while the diaphragm is depicted at the bottom, separating the thoracic cavity from the abdominal cavity. The stomach is partially visible at the bottom right, with the left gastric artery and splenic artery labeled. The liver is shown in the lower left corner, with the common hepatic artery labeled. Other labeled arteries include the vertebral artery, 3rd right posterior intercostal artery, right bronchial artery, superior and inferior left bronchial arteries, and esophageal branches of the thoracic aorta. The cervical, thoracic, and abdominal parts of the esophagus are labeled, transitioning into the stomach at the bottom. The inferior phrenic arteries are labeled at the bottom left, and the celiac trunk and splenic artery are at the bottom right. The image uses red for the arteries and beige for the ribs and other structures." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_neck/s9> .
