<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p41_b2> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#caption> "The figure illustrates the venous system around the rectum and pelvic area, highlighting the inferior vena cava, inferior mesenteric vein, and various other veins and plexuses." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s10> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p41_b6> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/818983003> <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s10> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p41_b2> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The diagram features an orange background representing the pelvic cavity, with a large blue vein at the top left labeled as the inferior vena cava, branching into smaller veins. The inferior mesenteric vein is shown as a smaller blue vein extending downward from the inferior vena cava. The middle sacral vein is a small blue vein extending downward from the common iliac veins. The middle rectal vein branches off from the internal iliac vein, and the internal pudendal vein branches off from the middle rectal vein. The internal pudendal vein in the pudendal canal is a continuation of the internal pudendal vein within a shaded area. The internal and external rectal plexuses are networks of thin white lines within the orange region. Leader lines connect labels to the corresponding parts of the diagram, indicating the direction and location of the veins and plexuses." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s10> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/13c1cc0f0f8e> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#Statement> <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s10> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p41_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The large intestine is depicted in light pink, with arteries shown in red branching out from the main arteries to provide blood supply. The diagram includes labeled arrows and text pointing to different arteries. The Arc of Riolan is at the top, the Superior mesenteric artery is centrally positioned, and the Ascending branch of the left colic artery points to the ascending part on the left side. The Inferior mesenteric artery is below the Superior mesenteric artery, and the Marginal artery is near the bottom edge. The Left colic artery points to the left side, while the Sigmoid arteries are at the bottom right. The Common iliac artery is at the bottom left, and the Superior rectal artery points to the upper part of the rectum. The Median sacral artery is at the very bottom, and the Middle rectal artery is near the bottom, pointing to the middle part of the rectum. The Internal iliac artery is also at the bottom left, the Right colic artery is on the right side, the Middle colic artery is near the center, and the Ileocolic artery is near the bottom left, pointing to the junction between the ileum and the cecum. The labels are in black text, and arrows connect them to specific parts of the large intestine." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s10> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/dce09eeb618a> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#object> <http://snomed.info/id/244411005> <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s10> .
