<http://example.org/medkg/inst/assert/3d39b8234174> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#polarity> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#Uncertain> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s7> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p45_b5> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The illustration presents a detailed view of the abdominal cavity, with the diaphragm positioned at the top left as a large, dome-shaped muscle. The liver is depicted on the upper left side, appearing as a large, reddish-brown organ with a smooth surface. The lesser omentum is shown as a thin, translucent membrane extending from the lesser curvature of the stomach to the liver. The pancreas is located behind the stomach, appearing as a long, flat organ with a pinkish hue. The duodenum is represented as a curved tube extending from the pancreas, marking the beginning of the small intestine. The mesentery is illustrated as a double-layered fold of peritoneum that suspends the small intestine from the posterior abdominal wall. The transverse colon itself is shown as a curved tube extending horizontally across the abdomen. The greater omentum is depicted as a large, apron-like fold of peritoneum extending from the greater curvature of the stomach to the transverse colon. The small intestine is portrayed as a coiled tube extending from the duodenum, occupying much of the lower central portion of the abdominal cavity. The spine is visible on the right side of the image, providing a reference for the location of the organs relative to the body's midline. Various shades of red and orange are used to indicate different layers of tissue and organs, and the diagram is labeled with black text pointing to specific areas within the abdominal cavity." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s7> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/assert/fbd4573f4a3b> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#target> <http://snomed.info/id/10200004> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s7> .
