<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p78_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The image presents an anterior view of the abdominal cavity, with the liver and its surrounding structures clearly labeled. The fibrous capsule of the liver is depicted as a thin layer of connective tissue covering the liver. The outline of the ligamentum venosum is shown as a fibrous band connecting the liver to the diaphragm. The right and left hepatic ducts are illustrated as two ducts carrying bile from the liver, converging to form the common hepatic duct. The bile duct is shown transporting bile from the liver to the small intestine, while the gallbladder is depicted as a sac for storing and concentrating bile. The esophagus and stomach are also visible, with the esophagus connecting to the stomach. The hepatogastric and hepatoduodenal ligaments are illustrated, along with the lesser omentum connecting the stomach to the liver. The celiac trunk and common hepatic artery are shown branching off to supply blood to the liver and other nearby organs. Each structure is labeled with a leader line or arrow pointing to the relevant part." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s7> .
