<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p4_b9> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The illustration presents a cross-sectional view of the intestinal wall, with the innermost mucosa layer depicted in pink, featuring finger-like villi and crypts. Beneath the mucosa, the muscularis mucosae is shown in yellow, connecting to the green submucosa layer, which contains blood vessels, nerves, and connective tissues. The circular muscle layer, shown in red, forms the outer layer of the submucosa, while the longitudinal muscle, also in red, is the outermost layer of the intestinal wall. The serosa, the final layer, is depicted in brown, covering the longitudinal muscle. Villi extend outward from the mucosa, and crypts are visible as invaginations within it. Arrows point to specific structures: villi, crypts, Peyer’s patch, lymph vessel, submucosal plexus, submucosal artery and vein, myenteric plexus, and the muscularis externa, which is highlighted in a red box." <urn:graph:digestive_system/s2> .
