<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p14_b3> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The figure consists of four distinct anatomical illustrations, each focusing on different aspects of the human body. The first illustration on the left displays the abdominal cavity with various organs labeled, including the liver, stomach, spleen, gallbladder, duodenum, jejunum, ileum, cecum, appendix, transverse colon, and sigmoid colon. The liver is positioned in the upper left quadrant, the stomach below it, the spleen to the right of the stomach, the gallbladder near the bottom left of the stomach, the duodenum extending from the stomach, the jejunum and ileum as coiled intestines below the duodenum, the cecum and appendix at the lower right, the transverse colon horizontally across the middle, and the sigmoid colon at the bottom right. The second illustration focuses on the digestive tract, excluding the first part of the duodenum, and highlights the duodenum, ascending colon, descending colon, and the part of the rectum proximal to the anorectal flexure. The third illustration emphasizes the urinary system and some abdominal structures, featuring the inferior vena cava as a blue vein running vertically through the center, the adrenal glands on either side of the kidneys, the kidneys on both sides of the spine, the ureters as tubes leading from the kidneys to the bladder, and the abdominal aorta as a red vessel running vertically through the center. The fourth illustration concentrates on the lower part of the urinary system." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s7> .
