<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p57_b9> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The image features a detailed anatomical illustration with the superior mesenteric artery positioned at the top-left. This artery is depicted as a blue and red vessel branching into smaller vessels that supply the small intestine. Below it, the jejunal and ileal arteries are shown as smaller branches extending downward along the length of the small intestine. The small intestine itself is portrayed in a coiled form, colored pinkish, with a network of blood vessels visible across its surface. The branches of the superior mesenteric artery are thin, red lines connecting to the intestinal wall. The overall layout has the superior mesenteric artery at the top, with the jejunal and ileal arteries extending downward, and the small intestine coiled with arterial branches running along its length." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s6> .
