<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p41_b3> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The illustration has a light beige background and features a title in red text at the top reading \"Arterial supply of the rectum and anal canal.\" The superior rectal artery is labeled at the top left with an arrow pointing to its main trunk. On the right, an arrow points to the right branch of the superior rectal artery. Further down, an arrow indicates the main descending branch of the superior rectal artery. Below this, the middle rectal artery is labeled with an arrow pointing to it. On the left side, below the middle rectal artery, the levator ani muscle is labeled with an arrow. On the right side, below the middle rectal artery, the terminal descending branches are labeled with an arrow. Below these, the ascending branches are labeled with an arrow pointing to them. At the bottom, the inferior rectal branch of the internal pudendal artery is labeled with an arrow, and finally, the anal canal is labeled at the very bottom with an arrow pointing to it. The diagram presents a cross-sectional view of the rectum and anal canal, with the arteries depicted in red branching out into smaller vessels, and the muscles and structures are labeled accordingly." <urn:graph:anatomy_of_pelvic_floor/s8> .
