<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p10_b10> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The external oblique muscle and its aponeurosis are positioned on the left side of the image, colored red, with an arrow pointing to it. On the right side, the membranous layer of superficial fascia, known as Scarpa's fascia, is shown in purple, also indicated by an arrow. Near the center, an arrow points to the area where the fascia continues with the superficial penile fascia, towards the lower abdomen. Another arrow on the right side indicates the attachment of the fascia to the fascia lata in the upper thigh area. An arrow near the center points to the attachment of the fascia to the ischiopubic rami, close to the pubic bones. At the bottom center, the superficial perineal fascia, or Colles' fascia, is depicted in green, with an arrow pointing to it. Finally, an arrow near the bottom left indicates the continuity between the superficial perineal fascia and the dartos fascia in the genital area." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s4> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/assert/2be99d103011> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#about> <http://snomed.info/id/84820005> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s4> .
