<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p22_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The figure is divided into two diagrams, labeled A and B. Each diagram provides a cross-sectional view, focusing on the abdominal wall muscles and fascia. The layout of the diagrams allows for a clear comparison between the two sections, with labels strategically placed to identify the different components of the anatomy." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s4> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p69_b6> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/32764006> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s4> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p46_b4> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The foregut is positioned on the left side of the image, the midgut in the center, and the hindgut at the bottom right. The superior mesenteric artery extends vertically downward on the right side, while the celiac trunk branches out from it to supply the foregut. The abdominal aorta is a large, vertical vessel on the far right, and the inferior mesenteric artery branches off from it to supply the hindgut. The stomach is located within the foregut region, the small intestine is coiled in the midgut area, and the large intestine is depicted as a straighter, larger tube in the hindgut region. The superior mesenteric artery is labeled at the top-right, the celiac trunk near the top-center, the abdominal aorta on the far right, and the inferior mesenteric artery at the bottom-right." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s4> .
