<http://example.org/medkg/inst/assert/da5048f581fa> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#polarity> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#Uncertain> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s9> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p74_b8> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#caption> "The figure illustrates the relative positions of the liver, diaphragm, subphrenic recess, hepatorenal recess, and kidney within the abdominal cavity." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s9> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p2_b2> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The diagram presents a coronal section of the human body, with the top-right corner featuring a legend that uses colors to differentiate body cavities: purple for the thoracic cavity, orange for the abdominal cavity, green for the greater (false) pelvis, blue for the lesser (true) pelvis, and light blue for the perineum. At the top of the diagram, the diaphragm is labeled, followed by the muscles of the abdominal wall below it. Near the top of the green area, the superior pelvic aperture is labeled, while the hip bone and head of femur are also indicated within this green section. Within the blue area, the obturator internus is labeled, and at the bottom, the pelvic diaphragm is identified. Muscles and bones are depicted in a simplified manner, showing their relative positions and connections." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s9> .
