<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p16_b11> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The image presents a cross-section of the human abdominal region, with the external oblique muscle prominently displayed in pink at the top. Beneath this, a lighter-colored muscle layer suggests the internal oblique. The pelvic region is shown at the bottom, featuring the sacrum and coccyx in light beige. The text \"(C) External oblique (EO)\" is positioned at the bottom, with \"(layer 1)\" directly beneath it." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s9> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p64_b2> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/59820001> <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s9> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p88_b4> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#caption> "The figure illustrates the human torso from the waist down, highlighting the median and transumbilical planes, the anterior superior iliac spine, the inguinal ligament, and the four quadrants of the abdomen." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s9> .
