<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p64_b2> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The illustration features a central structure representing the pelvis, with blood vessels branching out from it. The left common iliac artery is positioned on the left side, with arrows pointing from the top-left corner towards it. On the right side, the right common iliac artery is similarly situated, with arrows coming from the top-right corner. Below these, the left and right internal iliac arteries are positioned respectively, with arrows pointing towards them from the central structure. Within the central structure, the superior rectal artery branches off, while at the bottom, the inferior rectal artery, middle rectal artery, and internal pudendal artery are also depicted, each with arrows indicating flow direction." <urn:graph:abdominal_wall/s9> .
