<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p38_b8> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#caption> "The diagram illustrates the movement of ions and molecules across an intestinal cell membrane, highlighting the role of the Na+-K+-ATPase pump in maintaining ion balance." <urn:graph:digestive_system/s7> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p37_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The image is divided into two sections. In section (a), at the top left, there is a title that reads \"(a) Proteins are chains of amino acids.\" Below this, a diagram shows a chain of amino acids starting with an \"Amino-terminal end\" on the left, marked by a blue square containing \"H2N.\" The chain is composed of several blue squares connected by black lines, with triangular symbols labeled \"Peptide bonds\" pointing to the bonds between the amino acids. On the right, the chain ends with a \"Carboxy-terminal end,\" indicated by a yellow square with \"COOH\" inside. In section (b), the title at the top left reads \"(b) Enzymes for protein digestion.\" Below this, a flowchart explains protein digestion. A pink box states \"Endopeptidase digests internal peptide bonds,\" with an arrow pointing downward to a depiction of a protein chain being acted upon by an enzyme. The text mentions \"Endopeptidases include pepsin in the stomach, and trypsin and chymotrypsin in the small intestine,\" showing the protein chain breaking into smaller peptides. Another pink box says \"Exopeptidases digest terminal peptide bonds to release amino acids,\" with sub-boxes labeled \"Aminopeptidase\" and \"Carboxypeptidase.\" Arrows from these sub-boxes indicate the action on the protein chain, which breaks down into individual amino acids, each marked by an arrow." <urn:graph:digestive_system/s7> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/stmt/ac895ba30d10> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#confidence> "0.7"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#double> <urn:graph:digestive_system/s7> .
