<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p47_b7> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The figure is split into two sections: on the left, there's a photograph showing a person from the shoulders up, with short hair and facing forward. The neck area is highlighted by a red and blue object, likely a medical device for examining the thyroid gland. On the right, an anatomical diagram illustrates the neck's anatomy, with the thyroid gland colored light brown and outlined wavy. Red lines symbolize arteries, and blue lines symbolize veins. The cricoid cartilage is indicated by a black line pointing to the structure above the thyroid gland. The inferior thyroid artery is labeled with a black line pointing to an artery branching from the carotid artery, while the inferior thyroid veins are labeled with a black line pointing to a network of veins at the bottom of the thyroid gland." <urn:graph:histology_endocrine/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p62_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/18220004> <urn:graph:histology_endocrine/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p59_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#caption> "A microscopic cross-section of glandular tissue, showing various labeled structures including follicular cells, parafollicular cells, colloid, blood vessels, and connective tissue." <urn:graph:histology_endocrine/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/assert/dc1903367958> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#ClinicalAssertion> <urn:graph:histology_endocrine/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p62_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/264483005> <urn:graph:histology_endocrine/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p59_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#depicts> <http://snomed.info/id/50552001> <urn:graph:histology_endocrine/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p63_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#caption> "The figure presents a table detailing the composition, source, and major functions of the hormones thyroxine and calcitonin." <urn:graph:histology_endocrine/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p60_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#caption> "A detailed microscopic view of glandular tissue showing follicles, blood vessels, and connective tissue." <urn:graph:histology_endocrine/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p61_b8> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "A light blue area at the top represents the colloid, with green structures labeled \"1\" indicating the Golgi apparatus involved in thyroglobulin synthesis. Red arrows show the direction of thyroglobulin movement from the Golgi apparatus into the follicle lumen. At the bottom left, the iodide symporter (NIS) is depicted with yellow dots labeled \"Iodide\" showing iodine ions entering the cell. Near the top, thyroid peroxidase is shown with a leader line pointing to its location on the cell membrane. Orange circles at the top right represent MIT and DIT, connected by leader lines to thyroid peroxidase, indicating their role in iodination. Pre-T3 and Pre-T4 are also shown as orange circles at the top right, connected by leader lines to thyroid peroxidase, indicating their formation from MIT and DIT. Small, dark purple structures within the cell represent lysosomes, with leader lines pointing to them from colloid reabsorption droplets. Colloid reabsorption droplets are depicted as small, light purple structures within the cell, with leader lines pointing to them from the lysosomes." <urn:graph:histology_endocrine/s5> .
<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p62_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The diagram is organized with the Central Nervous System centers at the top left, followed by the hypothalamus positioned just below. The thyroid hormones T4 and T3 are represented by labeled boxes throughout the diagram, with arrows indicating their conversion in astrocytes and tanycytes. Near the blood-brain barrier, OATP is shown connected to the hypothalamus and other parts. The blood-brain barrier itself is depicted as a circular structure with a double-headed arrow, signifying its role in transport. Centrally located is the anterior lobe of the pituitary gland, which is connected to the hypothalamus and contains labeled hormones like TSH, TRH, and somatostatin. The thyroid gland is situated at the bottom center, linked to the pituitary gland by arrows. At the bottom right, target organs such as the liver and kidneys are shown, with arrows indicating T4 to T3 conversion. A legend on the left explains that red lines signify inhibitory signals, while blue lines represent stimulatory signals. Prolactin is shown as an arrow from the anterior pituitary to the breast. At the very bottom right, labels describe the effects of thyroid hormones: increased growth, development, metabolism, and body heat production." <urn:graph:histology_endocrine/s5> .
